---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
import OverlaysBrowser from '../../../../components/OverlaysBrowser.tsx';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const baseNorm = base.replace(/\/$/, '');
const dataUrl = `${baseNorm}/data/overlays.json`;
const detailHrefBase = `${baseNorm}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/overlays/`;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Per-overlay catalog"
  description="One descriptive page per ARM9 overlay: what it does at runtime, what assets it loads, which Japanese strings live in its binary, and the functions Ghidra found inside it."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'How the Game Works', href: `${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/` },
    { label: 'Per-overlay catalog' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Tongari Boushi's code is split into <strong>180 ARM9 overlays</strong> —
    small chunks of compiled ARM code that the DS swaps into a shared RAM slot
    as the player moves between game modes. Each overlay handles one screen,
    one feature, or one engine subsystem. This catalog is the per-overlay
    field guide: one page per overlay, describing what it does at runtime,
    what container files it pulls from disk, which UTF-16 LE strings are
    baked into its own binary, and the function table Ghidra's
    auto-analysis pass found inside it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href={`${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/overlays-system/`}>overlay
    system</a> page covers the runtime mechanics (RAM slot map, the
    controller/body pattern, the persistent rendering engine in ov137). The
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/overlay-knowledgebase/`}>overlay
    knowledgebase</a> page is the plain-English "what's an overlay?"
    explainer. This page is the searchable per-overlay index.
  </p>

  <h2>How the catalog reads</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Overlay</strong> — the ROM ID (ov000 through ov179). Click
      through to the per-overlay page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Purpose</strong> — a one-line summary drawn from the
      container files the overlay references. The small pill is the
      prior-pass role guess (the rough "what is this for" tag we had
      before the deep Ghidra pass).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fns</strong> — function count Ghidra auto-identified. Many
      are small trampolines; the dispatcher and high-fan-out functions are
      the meaty ones.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>JP strings</strong> — UTF-16 LE strings baked into the
      overlay's own binary. These live inside the compiled code section
      itself, not in an external container file.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Containers</strong> — distinct container basenames (looking
      like <code>buyitem.lzs</code>, <code>chest.lzs</code>) the overlay
      references by string. These are the disk-side data files the
      overlay loads at runtime.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What's an overlay made of</h2>
  <p>
    Each overlay is a self-contained ARM module with five rough contents:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Code</strong> — ARM/THUMB functions. Most overlays are
      almost-entirely code; the actual translatable text lives in
      container files loaded at runtime.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data tables</strong> — fixed-stride sprite-placement
      tables, dispatch tables, type tables. Read-only at runtime.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Container references</strong> — ASCII strings naming
      <code>.lzs</code>, <code>.ofs</code>, <code>.nftr</code> files the
      overlay's loader calls into the content cache for. The string is
      passed to a lookup that resolves it to a file ID in the ROM's FAT.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Embedded UTF-16 LE strings</strong> — direct text, baked
      into the overlay binary itself rather than loaded from a container.
      These are typically short menu labels and confirm prompts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>External call targets</strong> — addresses in the persistent
      ARM9 binary the overlay branches into for shared helpers (memory
      management, OAM transfer, content cache lookups).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>RAM-slot map at a glance</h2>
  <p>
    Overlays sharing a RAM address are mutually exclusive — only one is
    resident at a time. The dispatcher in the persistent ARM9 binary chooses
    which overlay to load into each slot based on game-mode state.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>RAM address</th><th># overlays</th><th>Overlay IDs</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0x02155a00</code></td><td>66</td><td>ov000–ov065</td><td>Game-mode controllers</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021597a0</code></td><td>25</td><td>ov066–ov090</td><td>Renderer / body slot 1</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0215c540</code></td><td>20</td><td>ov091–ov110</td><td>Renderer / body slot 2 (incl. NPC chat menu)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021fb3c0</code></td><td>18</td><td>ov160–ov177</td><td>Renderer / body slot 3 (incl. late-game extras)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021b1ba0</code></td><td>11</td><td>ov145–ov155</td><td>UCC / dress-up renderers</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0215e4a0</code></td><td>10</td><td>ov111–ov120</td><td>Renderer / body slot 5</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021a5ee0</code></td><td>6</td><td>ov139–ov144</td><td>Renderer / body slot 6</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021601c0</code></td><td>5</td><td>ov121–ov125</td><td>Renderer / body slot 7</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0216d120</code></td><td>5</td><td>ov132–ov136</td><td>Audio / system slot</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0216cba0</code></td><td>4</td><td>ov128–ov131</td><td>System slot</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0216da40</code></td><td>2</td><td>ov137, ov138</td><td>Rendering engine (2D / 3D map)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021b6080</code></td><td>2</td><td>ov156, ov157</td><td>Slot 4 bodies</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021c3420</code></td><td>2</td><td>ov158, ov159</td><td>Slot 5 bodies</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x02161680</code></td><td>2</td><td>ov126, ov127</td><td>Map / world</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x021fd2c0</code></td><td>2</td><td>ov178, ov179</td><td>Tail bodies</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Functional groupings</h2>
  <p>
    Inferred from substring-keyword matches on container filenames plus the
    deep Ghidra pass. Boundaries are approximate.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>2D UI screens</strong> — ov000–ov065 (game-mode controllers) and the slot-1/2/3 body renderers behind them. Shops, menus, chat panels.</li>
    <li><strong>UCC / dress-up</strong> — ov108, ov141, ov148–ov155. Outfit-composition surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Map / world</strong> — ov080, ov115–ov117, ov127, ov138, ov156. Field navigation, world map.</li>
    <li><strong>Persistent engines</strong> — ov137 (2D rendering), ov138 (3D map engine). Co-located at <code>0x0216da40</code>; one resident at a time.</li>
    <li><strong>Audio / system</strong> — ov132–ov136.</li>
    <li><strong>Pure code / dispatch</strong> — many of ov018–ov024, ov094–ov099, ov102–ov107, ov128–ov136, ov160–ov179 have no embedded strings and no container references; they are pure logic.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Per-overlay catalog</h2>

  <OverlaysBrowser dataUrl={dataUrl} detailHrefBase={detailHrefBase} client:load />

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>notes/_overlay_knowledgebase.md</code> — high-level overlay survey.</li>
    <li><code>notes/_overlay_knowledgebase.csv</code> — flat CSV of the same.</li>
    <li><code>notes/overlay_knowledgebase/ov&lt;NN&gt;.md</code> — one descriptive markdown per overlay.</li>
    <li><code>notes/overlay_knowledgebase/raw/ov&lt;NN&gt;.json</code> — raw Ghidra dump per overlay.</li>
    <li><code>notes/phaseA_ghidra/overlay_role_final.json</code> — per-overlay role classification.</li>
    <li><code>notes/phaseA_ghidra/container_to_overlay.json</code> — reverse map: container basename → overlays referencing it.</li>
    <li><code>notes/phaseA_ghidra/overlay_string_inventory.json</code> — every overlay-embedded string with offset and encoding.</li>
    <li><code>notes/phaseA_ghidra/DISCOVERIES.md</code> — aggregated reverse-engineering log.</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
